{
  "concept": "A projectile's flight path is a function h(x) of horizontal distance, and whether a hit clears an outfield wall reduces to comparing the ball's height to the wall's height at one x-value.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "x-axis labeled 'Distance (ft)', 0–600 with ticks every 100",
    "y-axis labeled 'Height (ft)', labeled ticks at 100, 200, 300",
    "faint dotted gridlines across the plotting area",
    "teal parabolic trajectory curve from origin, peaking just above 150 ft near x ≈ 290 ft, landing past 560 ft",
    "small arrowheads along the curve indicating direction of travel",
    "annotation 'Position of hitter' with leader line to the origin",
    "annotation 'Trajectory of ball' with leader line to a circular marker on the rising branch near x = 200",
    "red rectangular 'Outfield wall' marker standing on the x-axis, labeled",
    "movable ball marker on the curve with height readout and vertical drop line to the x-axis",
    "clearance highlight at the wall position (ball-above vs ball-below indicator)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "ballDistance",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Distance from hitter (ft)",
      "range": [0, 570, 5],
      "default": 200,
      "teaches": "Each distance along the ground corresponds to exactly one height on the curve — the trajectory is the graph of a function, and dragging the marker makes the (distance, height) pairs concrete"
    },
    {
      "id": "wallPosition",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Outfield wall position (ft)",
      "range": [100, 570, 5],
      "default": 400,
      "teaches": "Clearing the wall is a comparison of two heights at the same distance: the hit is a home run only where the trajectory is above the top of the wall"
    },
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["Smooth path", "Sampled points"],
      "default": "Smooth path",
      "teaches": "The continuous arc and a table of discrete distance–height samples are two representations of the same projectile motion"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Read the trajectory",
      "narration": "Drag the ball marker along the arc: at each distance from the hitter, the readout gives the ball's height. The curve climbs to a peak just over 150 ft near 290 ft, then falls.",
