    },
    {
      "title": "Does it clear the wall?",
      "narration": "With the wall at 400 ft, the ball is still well above it — the figure highlights the clearance. Slide the wall right toward 550 ft and watch the margin shrink until the ball arrives below the top of the wall.",
      "state": { "ballDistance": 400, "wallPosition": 400, "viewMode": "Smooth path" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Same motion, sampled",
      "narration": "Switch to sampled points: the smooth arc becomes discrete distance–height pairs on the grid — the same function you would see in a table of values.",
      "state": { "ballDistance": 400, "wallPosition": 400, "viewMode": "Sampled points" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Whether the hit is a home run is decided by a single comparison at the wall's distance: ball height from the trajectory versus wall height.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Chart.js line plot: x-axis 'Distance (ft)' 0–600 (ticks every 100), y-axis 'Height (ft)' 0–300+ with labeled ticks at 100/200/300 and light dotted grid. Teal downward-opening parabola from origin, apex slightly above 150 ft near x ≈ 290, crossing the axis just past 560. Directional arrowheads spaced along the curve. Leader-line annotations 'Position of hitter' → origin and 'Trajectory of ball' → a circled point on the rising branch near (200, 140). Red vertical rectangle for the outfield wall (movable via slider), label beneath. Ball marker slider draws a thin vertical drop line from the curve to the x-axis with a numeric height readout; at the wall's x-position show a green 'clears' or red 'caught/short' indicator comparing curve height to wall top. In sampled-points view, render the curve as discrete dots at regular x-intervals instead of a continuous line."
